// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Jemimah (Jerusalem Local)
// ACT: Act I
// CASE: The Missing Donkey
// CASE ID: triumphal_entry
// CASE_EXPORT: triumphal_entry
// SOURCE: act1_case_2d.js -> NPC 'local_skeptic'
// BIBLE REFERENCE: Matthew 21:10-11
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Zechariah 9:9
//     Gospel: Matthew 21:4-5; John 12:14-15
//     Insight: The donkey was not a practical choice - Roman rulers
//     rode horses. The donkey was a deliberate symbol of peace and
//     humility, contrasted with the conqueror's steed.
//   - Psalm 118:25-26
//     Gospel: Matthew 21:9; Mark 11:9-10
//     Insight: 'Hosanna' is the Greek transliteration of the Hebrew
//     'Hoshana' - a cry for salvation, not merely praise.
//   - Malachi 3:1
//     Gospel: Mark 11:11
//     Insight: The 'coming to the temple' link connects the triumphal
//     entry with the Temple cleansing the following day - two acts
//     that together announced Messiah's kingly authority and
//     prophetic mission.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

-> start

=== start ===
The whole city is in an uproar because of these country pilgrims.
They're tracking mud everywhere and throwing their dirty garments
in the middle of the road! I have work to do, and they are
blocking the eastern valley gate with their procession.

* [Who do people say this Galilean is?] -> who_is_he
* [The crowds are very devoted.] -> crowd_devotion
* [Is this not a religious celebration?] -> religious_celebration

=== who_is_he ===
Everyone is yelling "Hosanna!" from the tops of their lungs.
I heard one priest mutter that the whole world has gone after
Him. They were scribbling notes, frowning. I know nothing good
comes from Nazareth — I'll tell you that much. Nothing good
ever comes from Galilee.

* [But the people believe.] -> people_believe
* [The priests are concerned.] -> priest_concern
* [A prophet from Galilee?] -> prophet_from_galilee

=== crowd_devotion ===
People were cutting palm branches off the trees toward Jericho
and dragging them all the way up the path. The noise was
deafening — hundreds of voices shouting the same words. Cloaks
spread on the road. It was a miracle no one was blinded by
the flying palm fronds.

* [The cloaks were expensive.] -> expensive_cloaks
* [The palms carry political weight.] -> palm_politics
* [This is more than a welcome.] -> more_than_welcome

=== religious_celebration ===
Is this a religious celebration? Yes, technically. It is
Passover season. But this — this is not worship. This is
political theater. A man rides into the city on a borrowed
donkey, and suddenly everyone declares him king.

* [The king they want is different.] -> different_king
* [Political theater has consequences.] -> political_consequences
* [The religious meaning matters.] -> religious_meaning

=== people_believe ===
Believe? Yes. People who have walked with this Teacher for
three years believe He is the Messiah. They believe He can
raise the dead. They believe He speaks with authority.
But belief without understanding is dangerous. These
people are inviting Roman intervention.

* [Roman intervention means violence.] -> roman_violence
* [The people misunderstand.] -> people_misunderstand
* [They invite consequences.] -> invite_consequences

=== priest_concern ===
The priests are concerned — and rightly so. A crowd this
size, declaring a man to be king, is a threat to the
established order. The Sanhedrin knows what Rome does
to threats. The people do not.

* [The priests should guide, not suppress.] -> guide_vs_suppress
* [The Sanhedrin fears losing power.] -> fear_power
* [Both sides have valid concerns.] -> valid_concerns

=== prophet_from_galilee ===
A prophet? Maybe. But prophets do not usually ride into
town with an unauthorised parade. They do not gather
crowds large enough to shake the city gates. And they
most certainly do not provoke the Sanhedrin into emergency
sessions the way this one does.

* [He is more than a prophet.] -> more_than_prophet
* [The Sanhedrin reacts too quickly.] -> reacts_quickly
* [Prophets are meant to provoke.] -> prophets_provoke

=== expensive_cloaks ===
The cloaks they spread on the road — some were fine linen,
woven in Galilee. Others were wool dyed purple. These
were not beggars. These were people who had traveled
from distant lands, sacrificing their possessions to
honor this Teacher. The value of those garments scattered
in the dust was considerable.

* [Sacrificial giving.] -> sacrificial_giving
* [The cost showed devotion.] -> cost_devotion
* [Wasteful or worshipful?] -> wasteful_worshipful

=== palm_politics ===
Palm branches are not innocent decorations. The last time
Jerusalem waved palms in the streets was when Simon
Maccabaeus liberated the city from the Seleucids. The
crowd knows exactly what they are invoking. This is not
a greeting — it is a coronation gesture.

* [The Maccabean precedent.] -> maccabean_precedent
* [Coronation, not celebration.] -> coronation_not
* [The political message is clear.] -> political_message

=== more_than_welcome ===
This is more than a welcome. It is a demand. A declaration
that the existing order must yield to a new king. The
priests understood immediately. The soldiers on the walls
tensed. Even the merchants paused in their work.

* [The atmosphere changed.] -> atmosphere_changed
* [Everyone sensed the shift.] -> sensed_shift
* [History was being made.] -> history_made

=== roman_violence ===
Roman violence? Yes. The Praetorian guard has five
cohorts stationed around Jerusalem for Passover. One
spark, one false rumor, and they will flood these streets
with steel. The last time crowds gathered like this,
forty thousand died in the streets.

* [The memory of past massacres.] -> past_massacres
* [The priests prevent this.] -> priests_prevent
* [Fear of repetition.] -> repeat_fear

=== people_misunderstand ===
They misunderstand the nature of kingdoms. This Teacher
speaks of love and forgiveness, not of armies and conquest.
He speaks of a kingdom that is "not of this world." How
does a kingdom that is not of this world defend itself
from Rome?

* [The kingdom is spiritual.] -> kingdom_spiritual
* [Not of this world is not a political strategy.] -> not_strategy
* [The misunderstanding grows.] -> misunderstanding_grows

=== invite_consequences ===
By declaring this Teacher king, the people invite
consequences they cannot control. Rome does not tolerate
rival kings. Not even symbolic ones. Not even on donkeys.

* [The consequences arrive.] -> consequences_arrive
* [They cannot control Rome.] -> cannot_control
* [The price of defiance.] -> price_defiance

=== different_king ===
The king they want is a general. A conquering hero
who will drive the Romans from the city. The king
they got rides on a donkey. The king they got
speaks of turning the other cheek. The king they
got will die on a cross.

* [The disappointment will be great.] -> great_disappointment
* [They wanted power, not peace.] -> power_not_peace
* [The prophecy was different.] -> different_prophecy

=== political_consequences ===
Political theater has consequences. The priests
know this. The soldiers know this. The merchants
know this. Only the crowd — drunk on enthusiasm —
fails to see what they are inviting.

* [The crowd is blind to danger.] -> crowd_blind_danger
* [Enthusiasm breeds ignorance.] -> enthusiasm_ignorance
* [The danger is real.] -> danger_real

=== religious_meaning ===
The religious meaning matters. This is not merely
political theater. The crowd recognizes something
deeper — something that connects Zechariah's prophecy
to the present moment. They see the fulfillment.
They see the king.

* [The religious insight is valid.] -> religious_valid
* [But the political consequence is real.] -> political_real
* [Faith without wisdom.] -> faith_without_wisdom

=== guide_vs_suppress ===
The priests should guide, not suppress. But
suppression is easier than guidance. Suppression
is quieter than confrontation. Suppression
preserves the system that has served Israel
for centuries.

* [Preservation through suppression.] -> preservation_suppression
* [The system fears truth.] -> system_fears
* [Guidance requires courage.] -> guidance_courage

=== fear_power ===
The Sanhedrin fears losing power. Of course they do.
Power is not surrendered willingly. It is not
challenged without consequence. And this Teacher
is challenging the very foundation of their
authority.

* [Power without wisdom is tyranny.] -> power_tyranny
* [The challenge is theological.] -> challenge_theological
* [They fear God more than Rome.] -> fear_god_more

=== valid_concerns ===
Both sides have valid concerns. The people seek
salvation. The priests seek stability. The soldiers
seek order. Each perspective has merit. But merit
does not resolve the fundamental conflict.

* [The conflict cannot be resolved.] -> conflict_unresolvable
* [One perspective must dominate.] -> must_dominate
* [The price is paid by the innocent.] -> innocent_pay

=== more_than_prophet ===
He is more than a prophet. He is a claim. A
challenge. A declaration that the existing
order — priesthood, sacrifice, Temple —
must yield to something greater. Something
that the priests cannot control.

* [The claim is kingly.] -> claim_kingly
* [The challenge is to the system.] -> challenge_system
* [Something greater indeed.] -> greater_indeed

=== reacts_quickly ===
The Sanhedrin reacts quickly because they
understand the implications. A crowd
declaring a man to be king is not a
religious observation — it is a political
declaration. And political declarations
require immediate response.

* [The response should be measured.] -> response_measured
* [Political declarations threaten peace.] -> threat_peace
* [They respond with fear, not wisdom.] -> fear_not_wisdom

=== prophets_provoke ===
Prophets are meant to provoke. To challenge.
To disturb the comfortable. But this Teacher
provokes in a way that threatens the very
existence of the system. Prophets can be
managed. Kings cannot.

* [The distinction matters.] -> distinction_matters
* [Kings are harder to manage than prophets.] -> harder_manage
* [The system's limit is reached.] -> system_limit

=== sacrificial_giving ===
The value of those garments scattered
in the dust was considerable. Some
were ancestral heirlooms. Others were
the savings of widows. To lay them
down for a Teacher from Nazareth —
that is faith beyond measure.

* [Faith beyond measure.] -> faith_measure
* [The giving was voluntary.] -> giving_voluntary
* [The value was recognized.] -> value_recognized

=== cost_devotion ===
The cost showed devotion. No one
spreads a purple cloak on the ground
without understanding its value.
No one offers their wedding garment
for a stranger without knowing
they are offering everything.

* [Everything was offered.] -> everything_offered
* [Devotion beyond calculation.] -> devotion_beyond
* [The measure of love.] -> measure_love

=== wasteful_worshipful ===
Wasteful or worshipful? The line
blurs when you are standing in
the road, watching people lay
their treasures at the feet of
a man who has never worn a crown
of gold.

* [The worship was genuine.] -> worship_genuine
* [The line between waste and worship.] -> waste_worship
* [Genuine worship cannot be wasteful.] -> cannot_waste

=== maccabean_precedent ===
The Maccabean precedent — Simon
entering Jerusalem with palm
branches and sacred song, after
driving out the Seleucid oppressors.
The crowd today knows this history.
They are invoking it deliberately.
This is political symbolism at
its most powerful.

* [The precedent inspires hope.] -> precedent_hope
* [The symbolism is dangerous.] -> symbolism_dangerous
* [The crowd invokes liberation.] -> crowd_liberation

=== coronation_not ===
Coronation, not celebration.
The palms are not decorations.
They are declarations. The
crowd is not rejoicing —
it is enthroning. And the
man on the donkey is not
refusing the crown.

* [The crown is offered and accepted.] -> crown_accepted
* [Enthronement through recognition.] -> enthronement_recognition
* [The symbolism cannot be undone.] -> cannot_undo

=== political_message ===
The political message is
clear: we reject Roman rule.
We accept a different king.
One who comes in peace,
but whose peace will not
tolerate our oppression.
One who speaks to our
hearts, but whose justice
will one day speak to
our enemies.

* [Peace before justice.] -> peace_before_justice
* [The message spreads.] -> message_spreads
* [Romans cannot ignore this.] -> romans_ignore

=== atmosphere_changed ===
The atmosphere changed
the moment the donkey
entered the city gates.
The merchants stopped
their bargaining. The
soldiers straightened
their spines. The
priests exchanged glances
they did not want seen.

* [Everyone sensed the shift.] -> sensed_shift2
* [The city held its breath.] -> held_breath
* [History was arriving.] -> history_arriving

=== sensed_shift ===
Everyone sensed the
shift. Not just the
religious. Not just
the political. Everyone.
The beggars paused
in their begging.
The children forgot
their games. The
elderly leaned forward
on their staffs to
see the procession.

* [The whole city watched.] -> whole_city
* [A moment of recognition.] -> recognition_moment
* [All of Jerusalem stood still.] -> jerusalem_still

=== history_made ===
History was being
made. Not by kings
and conquerors.
Not by armies and
legions. But by a
crowd of ordinary
people who had
recognized something
 extraordinary
in an ordinary man
from Nazareth.

* [Ordinary means, extraordinary ends.] -> ordinary_extraordinary
* [God uses the simple.] -> god_simple
* [The moment was sacred.] -> moment_sacred

=== past_massacres ===
The memory of past
massacres — the
Sicarii's revolt, the
bandits' uprising, the
zealots' final stand.
Forty thousand
dead. Streets
flooded with blood.
The priests
remember. The
soldiers remember.
The people...
the people forget.

* [They forget at their peril.] -> peril_forget
* [Memory should instruct.] -> memory_instruct
* [The pattern repeats.] -> pattern_repeat

=== priests_prevent ===
The priests prevent
this. They manage
the crowds. They
negotiate with
Rome. They keep
the peace. But
today, they could
not prevent the
crowd's declaration.
They could only
watch and wait
for the
consequences.

* [The consequences arrive.] -> consequences_arrive2
* [They cannot prevent faith.] -> cannot_prevent_faith
